\chapter{Negative binomial regression}

\section{Where it comes from}

Chapter 7 showed that mixing Poisson distributions with varying rates produces
something more variable than a Poisson. Make that precise: let each unit have its
own rate $\lambda_i$, drawn from a gamma distribution with mean $\mu_i$, and let
the count be Poisson given that rate:
\[
Y_i \mid \lambda_i \sim \text{Poisson}(\lambda_i), \qquad
\lambda_i \sim \text{Gamma}(\theta,\ \theta/\mu_i).
\]
Integrate out $\lambda_i$ and you get exactly the negative binomial:
\[
\E(Y_i) = \mu_i, \qquad \Var(Y_i) = \mu_i + \frac{\mu_i^2}{\theta}.
\]

\begin{plainenglish}[The interpretation of theta]
$\theta$ measures how much units differ from one another beyond what the
predictors explain. Large $\theta$ (say above 20) means units are nearly
interchangeable and the model collapses back to a Poisson. Small $\theta$ (below 1)
means huge unexplained heterogeneity --- lots of zeros and a very long tail.
\end{plainenglish}

<<theta-shapes, fig.width=9.4, fig.height=3.4, out.width="\\textwidth", fig.cap="The same mean of 5 under four values of theta. As theta shrinks, zeros pile up and the tail stretches out.">>=
par(mfrow = c(1, 4)); par_book()
for (th in c(50, 5, 1, 0.3)) {
  k <- 0:30
  barplot(dnbinom(k, mu = 5, size = th), names.arg = k, border = "white",
          col = adjustcolor(PAL["accent"], 0.75), cex.names = 0.55,
          ylim = c(0, 0.45), main = bquote(theta == .(th)),
          xlab = "count", ylab = if (th == 50) "probability" else "")
}
@

\begin{warningbox}[Names for the same parameter]
\R{} calls it \fn{theta} (\pkg{MASS}) or \fn{size} (\fn{rnbinom}, \fn{dnbinom}).
Stata calls its reciprocal \fn{alpha}, so $\alpha = 1/\theta$. Some textbooks use
$k$. They all describe the same thing, but \emph{small} $\theta$ means the same as
\emph{large} $\alpha$. Check which convention a paper is using before comparing
numbers.
\end{warningbox}

\section{Fitting it}

<<nb-fit>>=
library(MASS)
data(quine)
m_nb <- glm.nb(Days ~ Eth + Sex + Age + Lrn, data = quine)
summary(m_nb)
@

The important extra line is \fn{Theta} at the bottom, with its own standard error.

<<nb-theta>>=
c(theta = m_nb$theta, se = m_nb$SE.theta, alpha = 1 / m_nb$theta)
@

With $\hat\theta = \Sexpr{round(m_nb$theta, 3)}$, the variance function is
$\mu + \mu^2/\Sexpr{round(m_nb$theta, 2)}$. At a fitted mean of 20, that is a
variance of
\Sexpr{round(20 + 400/m_nb$theta, 0)} instead of the Poisson's 20 --- roughly
\Sexpr{round((20 + 400/m_nb$theta)/20, 0)} times as variable.

\section{Poisson versus negative binomial, side by side}

<<nb-vs-pois>>=
m_pois  <- glm(Days ~ Eth + Sex + Age + Lrn, family = poisson, data = quine)
m_quasi <- glm(Days ~ Eth + Sex + Age + Lrn, family = quasipoisson, data = quine)

round(cbind(
  est_poisson = coef(m_pois),
  est_nb      = coef(m_nb),
  se_poisson  = coef(summary(m_pois))[, 2],
  se_quasi    = coef(summary(m_quasi))[, 2],
  se_nb       = coef(summary(m_nb))[, 2]
), 4)
@

Two things to notice. The coefficients move a little (the NB reweights the large
counts), and the standard errors roughly double or triple.

\subsection{Is the negative binomial actually better? The likelihood-ratio test}

The Poisson is the negative binomial in the limit $\theta \to \infty$, so the two
are nested and we can test between them:

<<nb-lrt>>=
LR <- 2 * (as.numeric(logLik(m_nb)) - as.numeric(logLik(m_pois)))
c(LR_statistic = LR,
  naive_p = pchisq(LR, df = 1, lower.tail = FALSE),
  correct_p = 0.5 * pchisq(LR, df = 1, lower.tail = FALSE))
@

\begin{keyidea}[Why the $p$-value is halved]
Under $H_0$ the extra parameter sits on the \emph{boundary} of its parameter space
($1/\theta = 0$ is the edge). The null distribution is then a 50:50 mixture of
$\chi^2_0$ and $\chi^2_1$, so the correct $p$-value is half the naive one. In
practice the evidence is usually so overwhelming that it makes no difference --- but
the halving is the technically right answer and it matters in borderline cases.
\end{keyidea}

\pkg{pscl} packages this test for you:

<<odtest>>=
pscl::odTest(m_nb)
@

\subsection{Compare on AIC too}

<<nb-aic>>=
round(c(AIC_poisson = AIC(m_pois), AIC_negbin = AIC(m_nb),
        BIC_poisson = BIC(m_pois), BIC_negbin = BIC(m_nb)), 1)
@

A drop of \Sexpr{round(AIC(m_pois) - AIC(m_nb))} AIC points is not a close call.

\section{Does it fit? Compare the predicted distributions}

The sharpest check for a count model is whether it can reproduce the shape of the
data.

<<nb-fitcheck, fig.width=9.4, fig.height=4.2, out.width="\\textwidth", fig.cap="Left: expected frequencies of each count under both models against what was observed. Right: a hanging rootogram -- bars that hang below zero are counts the model under-predicts.">>=
par(mfrow = c(1, 2)); par_book()
maxk <- 45
obs  <- as.numeric(table(factor(pmin(quine$Days, maxk), levels = 0:maxk)))
e_p  <- sapply(0:maxk, function(k) sum(dpois(k, fitted(m_pois))))
e_nb <- sapply(0:maxk, function(k) sum(dnbinom(k, mu = fitted(m_nb), size = m_nb$theta)))

plot(0:maxk, obs, type = "h", lwd = 3, col = adjustcolor(PAL["primary"], 0.6),
     xlab = "days absent", ylab = "frequency", main = "Observed vs expected")
grid_book()
lines(0:maxk, e_p,  lwd = 2.5, col = PAL["red"])
lines(0:maxk, e_nb, lwd = 2.5, col = PAL["green"])
legend("topright", bty = "n", cex = 0.8, lwd = c(3, 2.5, 2.5),
       col = c(adjustcolor(PAL["primary"], 0.6), PAL["red"], PAL["green"]),
       legend = c("observed", "Poisson", "negative binomial"))

## Hanging rootogram: bars hang from the fitted curve on a square-root scale
rootogram <- function(obs, exp_, main) {
  plot(range(0:(length(obs)-1)), c(-2.5, max(sqrt(exp_)) * 1.15), type = "n",
       xlab = "count", ylab = expression(sqrt("frequency")), main = main)
  grid_book()
  rect(0:(length(obs)-1) - 0.4, sqrt(exp_) - sqrt(obs),
       0:(length(obs)-1) + 0.4, sqrt(exp_),
       col = adjustcolor(PAL["primary"], 0.45), border = "white")
  abline(h = 0, lwd = 2, col = PAL["grey"])
  lines(0:(length(obs)-1), sqrt(exp_), lwd = 2.5, col = PAL["red"])
}
rootogram(obs, e_nb, "Rootogram: negative binomial")
@

\begin{plainenglish}[How to read a rootogram]
The red curve is what the model expects. Each bar hangs down from that curve by the
observed frequency. If the model is right, every bar just touches the zero line.
Bars ending \emph{below} zero mean the model under-predicts that count; bars
floating \emph{above} zero mean it over-predicts. It is the single most informative
plot for a count model.
\end{plainenglish}

\section{The two flavours: NB2 and NB1}

What we have fitted is \textbf{NB2}: variance $= \mu + \mu^2/\theta$, quadratic in
the mean. There is also \textbf{NB1}: variance $= \mu(1 + \alpha)$, linear in the
mean --- effectively quasi-Poisson but with a genuine likelihood. \pkg{glmmTMB}
fits both.

<<nb1-nb2>>=
library(glmmTMB)
nb2 <- glmmTMB(Days ~ Eth + Sex + Age + Lrn, family = nbinom2, data = quine)
nb1 <- glmmTMB(Days ~ Eth + Sex + Age + Lrn, family = nbinom1, data = quine)
round(c(AIC_nb2 = AIC(nb2), AIC_nb1 = AIC(nb1),
        AIC_glm.nb = AIC(m_nb)), 1)
@

\fn{glmmTMB} with \fn{nbinom2} reproduces \fn{glm.nb} exactly, as it should. Here
NB2 fits a little better than NB1, so the variance really does grow faster than
linearly.

\begin{rulebox}
Start with NB2 --- it is what everyone means by ``negative binomial'' and it is
what \fn{glm.nb} gives you. Try NB1 if the rootogram or the residual plots suggest
NB2 is over-correcting in the tail.
\end{rulebox}

\section{Reporting a negative binomial model}

<<nb-report>>=
tab <- cbind(IRR = exp(coef(m_nb)), exp(confint.default(m_nb)),
             p = coef(summary(m_nb))[, 4])
round(tab, 4)
@

\begin{keyidea}[The sentence]
``Aboriginal children were absent
\Sexpr{round(1/exp(coef(m_nb))["EthN"], 2)} times as often as non-Aboriginal
children (IRR \Sexpr{round(exp(coef(m_nb))["EthN"], 2)} for non-Aboriginal vs
Aboriginal, 95\% CI
\Sexpr{round(exp(confint.default(m_nb))["EthN", 1], 2)}--\Sexpr{round(exp(confint.default(m_nb))["EthN", 2], 2)}),
adjusting for sex, age group and learner status. A negative binomial model was used
because the counts were strongly overdispersed (Pearson dispersion
\Sexpr{round(sum(residuals(m_pois, type="pearson")^2)/df.residual(m_pois), 1)} under
a Poisson model); the estimated dispersion parameter was $\hat\theta =
\Sexpr{round(m_nb$theta, 2)}$.''
\end{keyidea}

Always state (a) that you checked for overdispersion, (b) what you found, (c) what
you did about it, and (d) the estimated $\theta$. Those four facts let a reader
judge your analysis.

\section{Things that go wrong}

\subsection{$\theta$ runs off to infinity}

If the data are not actually overdispersed, \fn{glm.nb} may fail to converge or
return a huge $\theta$ with a huge standard error. That is the model telling you a
Poisson would do.

<<theta-large>>=
set.seed(8)
d0 <- data.frame(x = rnorm(200)); d0$y <- rpois(200, exp(1 + 0.5 * d0$x))
suppressWarnings(nb0 <- glm.nb(y ~ x, data = d0))
c(theta = nb0$theta, se_theta = nb0$SE.theta)
@

An estimate that large with that much uncertainty means $1/\theta$ is
indistinguishable from zero. Use the Poisson.

\subsection{The negative binomial does not fix everything}

It handles \emph{extra variability}. It does not automatically handle
\emph{structural zeros}, and it does not handle clustering. Check the rootogram: if
only the zero bar is badly off while the rest fits, you need Chapter 10, not a
different variance function.

<<nb-zeros>>=
c(observed_zeros  = sum(quine$Days == 0),
  poisson_expects = round(sum(dpois(0, fitted(m_pois))), 1),
  negbin_expects  = round(sum(dnbinom(0, mu = fitted(m_nb), size = m_nb$theta)), 1))
@

Here the negative binomial predicts \Sexpr{round(sum(dnbinom(0, mu=fitted(m_nb),
size=m_nb$theta)), 1)} zeros against \Sexpr{sum(quine$Days == 0)} observed --- close
enough that zero-inflation is not needed for these data. The extra variability was
the whole problem, and the negative binomial has absorbed it.
